window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"AWS MLA Field Guide","section":"Build ML on AWS. Pass the exam.","tool":"home","text":"Build ML on AWS. Pass the exam. A sharp, visual path from basic ML vocabulary to Machine Learning Engineer – Associate (MLA-C02) — data prep, SageMaker modeling, Bedrock/RAG/agents, deploy, and operate. Constraint → architecture judgment. As of September 2026.","keywords":["build","ml","on","aws","pass","the","exam"]},{"url":"index.html#progress","page":"AWS MLA Field Guide","section":"Your progress","tool":"home","text":"01 Your progress Mark lessons \"learned\" as you go — progress stays in this browser. Resume anywhere or jump into a module below. Start learning Reset progress","keywords":["your","progress"]},{"url":"index.html#path","page":"AWS MLA Field Guide","section":"The journey, ML to exam","tool":"home","text":"02 The journey, ML to exam Learn the AWS ML map first, then decision trees mapped to the four MLA-C02 domains: Data prep · Model/FM development · Deploy & orchestrate · Operate, monitor, secure. Today’s path start here One sitting: ML lifecycle → SageMaker vs Bedrock → ingest → one scenario . Pictur","keywords":["the","journey","ml","to","exam"]},{"url":"index.html#modules","page":"AWS MLA Field Guide","section":"Explore the modules","tool":"home","text":"03 Explore the modules Six learning modules plus exam prep and reference. Start at Foundations if AWS ML is new. Start Foundations ML lifecycle on AWS, SageMaker vs Bedrock, exam method, responsible AI. Start -> Data 28% Data preparation Ingest, transform, bias/leakage, Feature Store, embeddings for","keywords":["explore","the","modules"]},{"url":"index.html#exam-surfaces","page":"AWS MLA Field Guide","section":"Exam surfaces","tool":"home","text":"04 Exam surfaces Domain map Weights, task verbs, and where each lesson maps. Scenario bank Constraint → architecture walkthroughs with Mermaid. Timed mock Domain-weighted items with why + lesson links.","keywords":["exam","surfaces"]},{"url":"foundations/ml-lifecycle-aws.html","page":"ML lifecycle on AWS","section":"The ML lifecycle on AWS — from raw data to monitored production.","tool":"foundations","text":"The ML lifecycle on AWS — from raw data to monitored production. MLA-C02 treats AWS as a pipeline of decisions : ingest → prepare → train → deploy → monitor. SageMaker is the training and classical-ML home; Bedrock sits at the GenAI layer for foundation models. As of September 2026, know where each ","keywords":["the","ml","lifecycle","on","aws","from","raw","data","to","monitored","production"]},{"url":"foundations/ml-lifecycle-aws.html#phases","page":"ML lifecycle on AWS","section":"Five phases, one mental map","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Five phases, one mental map Every exam scenario is a lifecycle question in disguise. Ask: which phase is broken? Fix that phase with the smallest managed AWS tool — not the flashiest one. In plain English analogy Think of ML like running a hospital lab. Ingest is receiving samples. Prep is cleani","keywords":["five","phases","one","mental","map"]},{"url":"foundations/ml-lifecycle-aws.html#acme","page":"ML lifecycle on AWS","section":"Acme Health — clinical readmission model","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Acme Health — clinical readmission model Acme Health predicts 30-day readmission risk from EHR extracts. The lifecycle maps cleanly to SageMaker — until they add a clinician Q&A chatbot, which pulls Bedrock into the deploy/monitor story. Acme Health · batch ML lifecycle ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× E","keywords":["acme","health","clinical","readmission","model"]},{"url":"foundations/ml-lifecycle-aws.html#studio","page":"ML lifecycle on AWS","section":"SageMaker Studio as the training home","tool":"foundations","text":"03 SageMaker Studio as the training home SageMaker Studio is the IDE; Training jobs , Processing jobs , and Pipelines are the production-grade workers. Notebooks explore — jobs scale. Studio / notebook Explore data, prototype features Data Wrangler visual flows Human-in-the-loop experiments Not the ","keywords":["sagemaker","studio","as","the","training","home"]},{"url":"foundations/ml-lifecycle-aws.html#rapid","page":"ML lifecycle on AWS","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Name the five ML lifecycle phases in order. warm-up Model answer Ingest → prepare → train → deploy → monitor. Monitoring feeds back into prep or retrain when drift or policy changes appear. Say it out loud say it \"Ingest, prep, train, deploy, monitor — and monitor closes the loop.","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"foundations/sagemaker-vs-bedrock.html","page":"SageMaker AI vs Bedrock","section":"Train a custom model or call a foundation model?","tool":"foundations","text":"Train a custom model or call a foundation model? MLA-C02 loves this fork: SageMaker when you need full control over algorithms, features, and artifacts; Bedrock when a managed FM plus prompt/RAG/fine-tune is enough. JumpStart is the middle ground — pretrained models you deploy or fine-tune without b","keywords":["train","custom","model","or","call","foundation"]},{"url":"foundations/sagemaker-vs-bedrock.html#chooser","page":"SageMaker AI vs Bedrock","section":"The chooser","tool":"foundations","text":"01 The chooser Start from the constraint : proprietary tabular fraud model with engineered features → SageMaker. Policy Q&A over PDFs with low ops → Bedrock + Knowledge Bases. Image classifier with a known pretrained backbone → JumpStart. flowchart TD start[\"New ML need\"] --> type{\"Problem type?\"} t","keywords":["the","chooser"]},{"url":"foundations/sagemaker-vs-bedrock.html#stories","page":"SageMaker AI vs Bedrock","section":"Ledger Bank vs Acme Health","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Ledger Bank vs Acme Health Same AWS account, different forks. Ledger's credit fraud model uses hand-crafted transaction features — SageMaker. Acme's discharge-summary chatbot uses Claude on Bedrock with a Knowledge Base over clinical guidelines. Ledger Bank · fraud scoring Millions of rows, stric","keywords":["ledger","bank","vs","acme","health"]},{"url":"foundations/sagemaker-vs-bedrock.html#jumpstart","page":"SageMaker AI vs Bedrock","section":"JumpStart — the middle ground","tool":"foundations","text":"03 JumpStart — the middle ground JumpStart bundles pretrained models, notebooks, and deploy scripts inside SageMaker. Use it when the exam mentions pretrained but still needs SageMaker endpoints — not Bedrock FMs. JumpStart Curated models (Hugging Face, built-ins) + one-click deploy/fine-tune in Sag","keywords":["jumpstart","the","middle","ground"]},{"url":"foundations/sagemaker-vs-bedrock.html#rapid","page":"SageMaker AI vs Bedrock","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire When is Bedrock the right default? warm-up Model answer GenAI workloads: text generation, summarization, chat, RAG over documents, agents — especially when the stem wants managed FMs and minimal training infrastructure. Say it out loud say it \"Bedrock when I need a foundation mode","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"foundations/exam-method.html","page":"How to take MLA-C02","section":"How to take MLA-C02 without drowning.","tool":"foundations","text":"How to take MLA-C02 without drowning. MLA-C02 is a timed scenario exam for ML engineers: underline constraints, map to a domain, eliminate wrong architectures. As of September 2026, verify format details on the official AWS certification page (beta code MLA-C02 ; predecessor MLA-C01 is retired). Typ","keywords":["how","to","take","mla","c02","without","drowning"]},{"url":"foundations/exam-method.html#domains","page":"How to take MLA-C02","section":"Know what you're graded on","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Know what you're graded on Four domains, nearly equal weight after data prep's slight lead. Train your eye for domain keywords before you fall in love with a service name. In plain English analogy Think of MLA-C02 as four station chiefs reviewing the same ML pipeline blueprint. One cares about cl","keywords":["know","what","you","re","graded","on"]},{"url":"foundations/exam-method.html#technique","page":"How to take MLA-C02","section":"Elimination technique","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Elimination technique Most stems hide one hard constraint that kills 2–3 options. Underline it. Then pick the simplest remaining AWS-native ML design. 1 Underline constraints Must , cannot , least operational overhead , latency ms, \"real-time,\" \"batch only,\" \"no public internet,\" \"PII,\" \"lowest c","keywords":["elimination","technique"]},{"url":"foundations/exam-method.html#study","page":"How to take MLA-C02","section":"What to memorize vs reason","tool":"foundations","text":"03 What to memorize vs reason Memorize sharp dichotomies (SageMaker vs Bedrock, online vs offline Feature Store, real-time vs batch inference). Reason about scenarios using lifecycle phase and domain weight. Memorize Four MLA-C02 domains and rough weights SageMaker jobs vs Studio notebooks for prod ","keywords":["what","to","memorize","vs","reason"]},{"url":"foundations/exam-method.html#rapid","page":"How to take MLA-C02","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire What are the four MLA-C02 domains and weights? warm-up Model answer Data preparation 28%; Model/FM development 24%; Deployment & orchestration 24%; Monitoring, security, and cost 24%. C01 is retired — study C02. Say it out loud say it \"28 data, 24 model, 24 deploy, 24 operate — fo","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"foundations/responsible-ai.html","page":"Responsible AI overview","section":"Build ML that is fair, explainable, and safe to ship.","tool":"foundations","text":"Build ML that is fair, explainable, and safe to ship. MLA-C02 expects you to spot bias, fairness gaps, transparency, and safety constraints — not as ethics essays, but as architecture choices. SageMaker Clarify and Bedrock Guardrails are the AWS-native levers; this lesson previews where they appear ","keywords":["build","ml","that","is","fair","explainable","and","safe","to","ship"]},{"url":"foundations/responsible-ai.html#pillars","page":"Responsible AI overview","section":"Four pillars on the exam","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Four pillars on the exam When a stem mentions protected attributes , explainability , harmful content , or regulatory audit , you are in responsible-AI territory. Pick the tool that matches the lifecycle phase. flowchart TD concern[\"RAI concern\"] --> bias{\"Bias / fairness?\"} bias -->|yes| clarify","keywords":["four","pillars","on","the","exam"]},{"url":"foundations/responsible-ai.html#stories","page":"Responsible AI overview","section":"Acme Health and Ledger Bank","tool":"foundations","text":"02 Acme Health and Ledger Bank Responsible AI shows up as constraints in both stories — not optional polish. Acme Health · readmission model Clarify checks bias on race/sex proxies before approval Clinicians need SHAP-style reason codes per prediction Chatbot gets Guardrails — no diagnosis, no PHI l","keywords":["acme","health","and","ledger","bank"]},{"url":"foundations/responsible-ai.html#tools","page":"Responsible AI overview","section":"Clarify and Guardrails — preview pointers","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Clarify and Guardrails — preview pointers You will go deeper in modeling and GenAI lessons. For MLA-C02 foundations, know when to reach for each — not every API parameter. SageMaker Clarify Pre-training bias metrics on datasets Post-training bias on model predictions SHAP explainability for tabul","keywords":["clarify","and","guardrails","preview","pointers"]},{"url":"foundations/responsible-ai.html#rapid","page":"Responsible AI overview","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire When do you use Clarify vs Guardrails? warm-up Model answer Clarify for dataset/model bias and SHAP explainability on SageMaker-trained models. Guardrails for Bedrock FM safety — toxic content, denied topics, PII on generated text. Say it out loud say it \"Clarify for bias and SHAP","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"data-prep/ingest-and-store.html","page":"Ingest & store for ML","section":"Land ML data in the right place — lake, catalog, stream.","tool":"data-prep","text":"Land ML data in the right place — lake, catalog, stream. Domain 1 starts here: S3 as the default lake, Glue Data Catalog for discoverable metadata, Kinesis / Firehose for streaming ingest. Pick Athena vs Processing based on whether you need one-off SQL or repeatable heavy transforms.","keywords":["land","ml","data","in","the","right","place","lake","catalog","stream"]},{"url":"data-prep/ingest-and-store.html#lake","page":"Ingest & store for ML","section":"S3 lake + Glue Catalog","tool":"data-prep","text":"01 S3 lake + Glue Catalog Raw ML data lives in S3 prefixes with lifecycle and encryption. Glue Crawlers and the Data Catalog make tables visible to Athena, Glue ETL, and SageMaker — without copying data into a proprietary warehouse first. In plain English analogy S3 is the warehouse floor where pall","keywords":["s3","lake","glue","catalog","raw","curated","features"]},{"url":"data-prep/ingest-and-store.html#chooser","page":"Ingest & store for ML","section":"Batch vs streaming ingest","tool":"data-prep","text":"02 Batch vs streaming ingest Nightly EHR dumps → S3 batch upload or DataSync. Live card swipes → Kinesis Data Streams or Firehose into S3/OpenSearch. Match velocity to the fraud or clinical use case. flowchart TD src[\"Data source\"] --> vel{\"Velocity?\"} vel -->|batch files| s3[\"S3 + Crawler\"] vel -->","keywords":["batch","vs","streaming","ingest"]},{"url":"data-prep/ingest-and-store.html#athena","page":"Ingest & store for ML","section":"Athena vs SageMaker Processing","tool":"data-prep","text":"03 Athena vs SageMaker Processing Athena = ad hoc SQL over cataloged S3 (explore, filter, aggregate). SageMaker Processing = repeatable, heavy transforms with custom code/containers feeding training. Glue ETL sits between for scheduled Spark jobs. Athena Serverless SQL on S3 via Glue Catalog Quick e","keywords":["athena","vs","sagemaker","processing"]},{"url":"data-prep/ingest-and-store.html#rapid","page":"Ingest & store for ML","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"data-prep","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Default store for ML raw data on AWS? warm-up Model answer S3 data lake with governed prefixes, encryption, and Glue Data Catalog metadata. Streaming sources feed in via Kinesis/Firehose. Say it out loud say it \"S3 lake + Glue Catalog — stream in with Kinesis or Firehose when even","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"data-prep/transform-and-features.html","page":"Transform & feature engineering","section":"Turn raw tables into train-ready features.","tool":"data-prep","text":"Turn raw tables into train-ready features. After ingest, ML quality depends on transforms : Glue ETL for Spark-scale jobs, SageMaker Processing for Python/sklearn pipelines, Data Wrangler for visual prototyping. Split train / validation / test before any leakage creeps in.","keywords":["turn","raw","tables","into","train","ready","features"]},{"url":"data-prep/transform-and-features.html#tools","page":"Transform & feature engineering","section":"Three transform paths","tool":"data-prep","text":"01 Three transform paths Pick by scale and repeatability: Glue for scheduled Spark ETL across the lake; Processing for SageMaker-native prep; Data Wrangler to explore then export to Processing or Pipelines. flowchart TD raw[\"Raw S3 data\"] --> need{\"Transform style?\"} need -->|Spark / SQL ETL| glue[\"","keywords":["three","transform","paths"]},{"url":"data-prep/transform-and-features.html#splits","page":"Transform & feature engineering","section":"Train, validation, test splits","tool":"data-prep","text":"02 Train, validation, test splits Split before feature engineering that learns from the full dataset (scaling stats, target encoding). Time-series? Split by time — never random shuffle on Ledger's fraud labels. 1 Train set Fit transforms and model weights. Largest slice — typically 60–80%. 2 Validat","keywords":["train","validation","test","splits"]},{"url":"data-prep/transform-and-features.html#exam-cues","page":"Transform & feature engineering","section":"Exam cues — Acme & Ledger","tool":"data-prep","text":"03 Exam cues — Acme & Ledger Stems hide the transform tool in operational words: \"nightly,\" \"same Python logic,\" \"visual then automate,\" \"10 TB join.\" Acme Health Glue joins EHR + labs → de-identified curated set Processing encodes categoricals, imputes vitals Time-based split: train on past admissi","keywords":["exam","cues","acme","ledger"]},{"url":"data-prep/transform-and-features.html#rapid","page":"Transform & feature engineering","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"data-prep","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Glue ETL vs SageMaker Processing? warm-up Model answer Glue = Spark-scale lake ETL, catalog integration, scheduled jobs across huge data. Processing = Python/sklearn/custom containers tightly coupled to SageMaker Training/Pipelines. Say it out loud say it \"Glue for lake-scale Spar","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"data-prep/integrity-bias-leakage.html","page":"Integrity, bias & leakage","section":"Clean data beats a fancier algorithm.","tool":"data-prep","text":"Clean data beats a fancier algorithm. Domain 1 heavy hitters: leakage (future info in training), label quality , and bias in datasets. SageMaker Clarify catches many issues pre-training — but only if your splits and features are designed correctly first.","keywords":["clean","data","beats","fancier","algorithm"]},{"url":"data-prep/integrity-bias-leakage.html#leakage","page":"Integrity, bias & leakage","section":"Leakage — the silent killer","tool":"data-prep","text":"01 Leakage — the silent killer Leakage means the model sees information at train time that won't exist at predict time. Metrics look amazing; production fails. Underline any feature derived from after the prediction moment . In plain English analogy Leakage is studying for a spelling test using the ","keywords":["leakage","the","silent","killer"]},{"url":"data-prep/integrity-bias-leakage.html#acme-story","page":"Integrity, bias & leakage","section":"Acme Health leakage story","tool":"data-prep","text":"02 Acme Health leakage story Acme's first readmission model scored 0.98 AUC in Studio — then failed in the ward. Root cause: \"days_until_followup\" was computed from post-discharge appointments, unavailable at admission scoring time. 1 Symptom Training metrics too good; live scores barely beat random","keywords":["acme","health","leakage","story"]},{"url":"data-prep/integrity-bias-leakage.html#labels-bias","page":"Integrity, bias & leakage","section":"Label quality & Clarify bias cues","tool":"data-prep","text":"03 Label quality & Clarify bias cues Garbage labels → garbage model. Clarify measures pre-training bias (dataset representation) and post-training bias (prediction disparities). Run both before Registry approval. Label issues Delayed fraud confirmations (Ledger) Mis-coded discharge statuses (Acme) C","keywords":["label","quality","clarify","bias","cues"]},{"url":"data-prep/integrity-bias-leakage.html#rapid","page":"Integrity, bias & leakage","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"data-prep","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire What is data leakage in one sentence? warm-up Model answer Training features contain information that would not be available at inference time (or that directly encodes the label), inflating offline metrics. Say it out loud say it \"Leakage is future or label info sneaking into tra","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"data-prep/feature-store.html","page":"Feature Store patterns","section":"One feature definition for training and serving.","tool":"data-prep","text":"One feature definition for training and serving. Train/serve skew breaks production ML. SageMaker Feature Store gives offline stores for training at scale and an online low-latency store for real-time inference — same feature name, same transform logic, consistent values.","keywords":["one","feature","definition","for","training","and","serving"]},{"url":"data-prep/feature-store.html#dual","page":"Feature Store patterns","section":"Online vs offline stores","tool":"data-prep","text":"01 Online vs offline stores Write features once via ingestion (Streaming or batch). Offline = historical S3-backed records for Training jobs. Online = millisecond lookups keyed by entity ID at endpoint invoke time. flowchart LR ingest[\"Feature ingestion\"] --> offline[\"Offline store · S3\"] ingest -->","keywords":["online","vs","offline","stores","ledger","card","features"]},{"url":"data-prep/feature-store.html#ledger","page":"Feature Store patterns","section":"Ledger Bank — fraud at swipe time","tool":"data-prep","text":"02 Ledger Bank — fraud at swipe time Ledger computes 200 transaction aggregates per card. Training reads historical offline records; the fraud endpoint pulls the same features from the online store in <10 ms. 1 Processing job Nightly batch computes velocity, merchant diversity, geo flags. 2 Ingest t","keywords":["ledger","bank","fraud","at","swipe","time"]},{"url":"data-prep/feature-store.html#when","page":"Feature Store patterns","section":"When the exam picks Feature Store","tool":"data-prep","text":"03 When the exam picks Feature Store Keywords: same features , real-time inference , historical training , train/serve consistency , multiple models share features . Skip it for one-off batch models with no online path. Common trap gotcha Feature Store for RAG embeddings. Vector embeddings for Bedro","keywords":["when","the","exam","picks","feature","store"]},{"url":"data-prep/feature-store.html#rapid","page":"Feature Store patterns","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"data-prep","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Offline vs online Feature Store? warm-up Model answer Offline = historical S3-backed data for training and batch jobs with point-in-time queries. Online = low-latency latest feature values keyed by entity ID for real-time inference. Say it out loud say it \"Offline for train histor","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"data-prep/embeddings-for-rag.html","page":"Embeddings & vector data for RAG","section":"Chunk, embed, index — the data path behind Knowledge Bases.","tool":"data-prep","text":"Chunk, embed, index — the data path behind Knowledge Bases. RAG quality is mostly data prep : split documents into chunks, embed with a Bedrock embedding model, store vectors in OpenSearch Serverless (or another supported vector store). Bedrock Knowledge Bases automates ingest → embed → retrieve for","keywords":["chunk","embed","index","the","data","path","behind","knowledge","bases"]},{"url":"data-prep/embeddings-for-rag.html#path","page":"Embeddings & vector data for RAG","section":"RAG data path","tool":"data-prep","text":"01 RAG data path At query time the FM doesn't memorize your PDFs — it retrieves similar chunks and grounds the answer. Bad chunking = wrong citations and hallucinations dressed as policy. flowchart LR docs[\"S3 docs / PDFs\"] --> chunk[\"Chunk · split\"] chunk --> embed[\"Bedrock embed model\"] embed --> ","keywords":["rag","data","path"]},{"url":"data-prep/embeddings-for-rag.html#chunking","page":"Embeddings & vector data for RAG","section":"Chunking decisions","tool":"data-prep","text":"02 Chunking decisions Smaller chunks = precise retrieval, less context per hit. Larger chunks = more context, muddier matches. Clinical protocols (Acme) often chunk by section header; regulatory PDFs (Ledger) by article number. Small chunks (~256 tokens) Better precision for narrow questions More in","keywords":["chunking","decisions"]},{"url":"data-prep/embeddings-for-rag.html#acme-kb","page":"Embeddings & vector data for RAG","section":"Acme Health Knowledge Base setup","tool":"data-prep","text":"03 Acme Health Knowledge Base setup Acme stores clinical guidelines in S3. A Knowledge Base syncs nightly, embeds with Titan Text Embeddings, indexes in OpenSearch Serverless. Clinicians query via Bedrock — citations point to source chunks. Acme · S3 docs to grounded answer ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× ","keywords":["acme","health","knowledge","base","setup"]},{"url":"data-prep/embeddings-for-rag.html#rapid","page":"Embeddings & vector data for RAG","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"data-prep","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire What are the three RAG data prep steps? warm-up Model answer Chunk documents, embed chunks with a Bedrock embedding model, store vectors in a vector database (OpenSearch Serverless with Knowledge Bases). At query time embed the question and retrieve top-K similar chunks. Say it ou","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"modeling/algorithm-choice.html","page":"Algorithm & problem framing","section":"Match the problem to the algorithm.","tool":"modeling","text":"Match the problem to the algorithm. MLA-C02 stems rarely ask you to derive math — they ask which AWS-friendly approach fits the data shape, interpretability need, and latency budget. Tabular vs text, linear vs tree ensemble, classic NLP vs foundation models: pick from constraints, not brand loyalty.","keywords":["match","the","problem","to","algorithm"]},{"url":"modeling/algorithm-choice.html#tabular","page":"Algorithm & problem framing","section":"Tabular: linear vs tree ensembles","tool":"modeling","text":"01 Tabular: linear vs tree ensembles Structured rows with mixed numeric/categorical features → start with linear models when you need speed and interpretability; reach for XGBoost / tree ensembles when interactions and non-linearity dominate and you have enough rows. In plain English analogy Linear ","keywords":["tabular","linear","vs","tree","ensembles"]},{"url":"modeling/algorithm-choice.html#cv-nlp","page":"Algorithm & problem framing","section":"Cross-validation & NLP classic vs FM","tool":"modeling","text":"02 Cross-validation & NLP classic vs FM Use k-fold cross-validation on tabular sets when data is limited and i.i.d.; hold out a final test set you never tune on. For NLP, classic pipelines (tokenize → TF-IDF → linear) still win on small, domain-specific keyword tasks; foundation models win on open-e","keywords":["cross","validation","nlp","classic","vs","fm"]},{"url":"modeling/algorithm-choice.html#quiz","page":"Algorithm & problem framing","section":"Exam chooser quiz","tool":"modeling","text":"03 Exam chooser quiz Acme Health must explain which vitals drive readmission scores for auditors. Data is tabular, 50k rows. Best first algorithm? Logistic regression / linear model with regularization — interpretable coefficients. Deep transformer fine-tuned from scratch on vitals only. XGBoost wit","keywords":["exam","chooser","quiz"]},{"url":"modeling/algorithm-choice.html#interview","page":"Algorithm & problem framing","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"modeling","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire When do you pick XGBoost over linear on tabular data? core Model answer When non-linear feature interactions matter, you have enough rows to avoid severe overfit, and interpretability is not the top constraint — or you pair trees with SageMaker Clarify for post-hoc explanations. S","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"modeling/train-on-sagemaker.html","page":"Train on SageMaker","section":"Train on SageMaker without over-building.","tool":"modeling","text":"Train on SageMaker without over-building. MLA-C02 wants you to pick the lightest training mode that fits: built-in algorithm, script mode, or bring-your-own container. Match instance family to workload; add distributed training or VPC only when the stem forces it.","keywords":["train","on","sagemaker","without","over","building"]},{"url":"modeling/train-on-sagemaker.html#modes","page":"Train on SageMaker","section":"Built-in vs script vs container","tool":"modeling","text":"01 Built-in vs script vs container Built-in algorithms for standard tabular/NLP/image jobs with minimal code. Script mode when you need custom loops but still want the managed Training Job API. BYOC when dependencies or entrypoints don’t fit the SageMaker contract. flowchart LR stem[\"Training need\"]","keywords":["built","in","vs","script","container","train","py"]},{"url":"modeling/train-on-sagemaker.html#instances","page":"Train on SageMaker","section":"Instance types & distributed training","tool":"modeling","text":"02 Instance types & distributed training CPU instances ( ml.m* , ml.c* ) for most tabular jobs. ml.g* / ml.p* when the stem mentions deep learning or large batches. Distributed = multiple instances only when single-node time or memory fails — exam answers prefer the simplest scale-up first. Signal i","keywords":["instance","types","distributed","training"]},{"url":"modeling/train-on-sagemaker.html#vpc-quiz","page":"Train on SageMaker","section":"VPC & exam quiz","tool":"modeling","text":"03 VPC & exam quiz Ledger Bank trains on CSV in a private S3 bucket; features must never traverse the public internet. Training reads only from that bucket and a private feature DB in the VPC. What do you add? SageMaker Training Job in VPC subnets + S3 gateway endpoint (or PrivateLink) + security gr","keywords":["vpc","exam","quiz"]},{"url":"modeling/train-on-sagemaker.html#interview","page":"Train on SageMaker","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"modeling","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Built-in vs script mode in one sentence? warm-up Model answer Built-in = AWS-owned algorithm + hyperparameters; script mode = your training script packaged for the same managed Training Job lifecycle. Say it out loud say it \"Built-in when the algo exists; script mode when I need c","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"modeling/hpo-and-tuning.html","page":"HPO & tuning","section":"HPO when it pays — skip when it doesn’t.","tool":"modeling","text":"HPO when it pays — skip when it doesn’t. SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning runs parallel training jobs over hyperparameter ranges. MLA stems test whether you know when Bayesian search beats grid search in your head — and when tuning is waste.","keywords":["hpo","when","it","pays","skip","doesn"]},{"url":"modeling/hpo-and-tuning.html#amt","page":"HPO & tuning","section":"SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning","tool":"modeling","text":"01 SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning Define an objective metric (validation AUC, loss), hyperparameter ranges , and max jobs / parallel jobs . SageMaker launches Training Jobs, tracks results, and suggests the next points — typically Bayesian for efficiency. flowchart LR cfg[\"Hyperparameter ranges\"] ","keywords":["sagemaker","automatic","model","tuning","validation","auc","max_depth","eta","subsample"]},{"url":"modeling/hpo-and-tuning.html#warm-start","page":"HPO & tuning","section":"Warm start & when NOT to HPO","tool":"modeling","text":"02 Warm start & when NOT to HPO Warm start seeds a new tuning job with results from a previous job — use when data drifted slightly or you narrowed ranges. Skip HPO when a baseline is enough, data is tiny, or the stem wants fast time-to-production. Use HPO when… Multiple hyperparameters materially a","keywords":["warm","start","when","not","to","hpo"]},{"url":"modeling/hpo-and-tuning.html#quiz","page":"HPO & tuning","section":"Exam quiz","tool":"modeling","text":"03 Exam quiz Team re-runs tuning monthly after retraining on refreshed data, reusing last month’s best hyperparameters as starting points. Which SageMaker feature? Warm start — new tuning job inherits prior job results. Hyperparameter tuning only works on first train ever. Must use Bedrock instead o","keywords":["exam","quiz"]},{"url":"modeling/hpo-and-tuning.html#interview","page":"HPO & tuning","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"modeling","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire What three things define an Automatic Model Tuning job? core Model answer Objective metric to maximize/minimize, hyperparameter ranges, and resource limits (max jobs / parallel jobs). Optional: early stopping and warm start. Say it out loud say it \"Metric, ranges, job budget — tha","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"modeling/evaluate-and-clarify.html","page":"Evaluate, metrics & Clarify","section":"Pick the metric that matches the mistake cost.","tool":"modeling","text":"Pick the metric that matches the mistake cost. MLA-C02 ties problem type to metrics — accuracy alone is rarely right. SageMaker Clarify adds bias and explainability when stems mention fairness, regulators, or “why was this denied?”","keywords":["pick","the","metric","that","matches","mistake","cost"]},{"url":"modeling/evaluate-and-clarify.html#metrics","page":"Evaluate, metrics & Clarify","section":"Metrics by problem type","tool":"modeling","text":"01 Metrics by problem type Classification with class imbalance → precision/recall/F1 , PR-AUC , or cost-weighted metrics — not raw accuracy. Regression → RMSE/MAE . Ranking → MAP/NDCG . Multiclass → macro vs weighted F1 depending on whether rare classes matter equally. Problem Metric cue Story Imbal","keywords":["metrics","by","problem","type"]},{"url":"modeling/evaluate-and-clarify.html#clarify","page":"Evaluate, metrics & Clarify","section":"SageMaker Clarify: bias & explainability","tool":"modeling","text":"02 SageMaker Clarify: bias & explainability Run Clarify on training data or endpoint predictions for pre-training bias , post-training bias , and SHAP feature importance . Use when stems cite protected attributes, disparate impact, or explainability to clinicians/customers. Pre-training bias Class i","keywords":["sagemaker","clarify","bias","explainability"]},{"url":"modeling/evaluate-and-clarify.html#holdout","page":"Evaluate, metrics & Clarify","section":"Holdout vs cross-validation","tool":"modeling","text":"03 Holdout vs cross-validation Holdout test set — untouched until final sign-off; reports production-realistic performance. Cross-validation — for model selection and HPO on limited data. Exam rule: tune on train/val (or CV folds), report once on holdout. Team used the test set to pick hyperparamete","keywords":["holdout","vs","cross","validation"]},{"url":"modeling/evaluate-and-clarify.html#interview","page":"Evaluate, metrics & Clarify","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"modeling","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Fraud detection metric — accuracy or something else? core Model answer Precision/recall trade-off, PR-AUC, or precision at a fixed recall — because negatives dominate and false positives have operational cost. Say it out loud say it \"Imbalanced classification → PR-AUC or precision","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"modeling/jumpstart-and-transfer.html","page":"JumpStart & transfer learning","section":"JumpStart vs train from scratch.","tool":"modeling","text":"JumpStart vs train from scratch. SageMaker JumpStart ships pretrained models, notebooks, and fine-tuning recipes. MLA stems contrast JumpStart fine-tune with Bedrock FMs — custom ML on SageMaker vs managed GenAI API.","keywords":["jumpstart","vs","train","from","scratch"]},{"url":"modeling/jumpstart-and-transfer.html#jumpstart","page":"JumpStart & transfer learning","section":"JumpStart pretrained & fine-tune","tool":"modeling","text":"01 JumpStart pretrained & fine-tune JumpStart gives pretrained weights (vision, NLP, tabular embeddings) plus one-click fine-tune/deploy. Fine-tune when your label distribution differs from pretraining; train from scratch only when data is huge and domain is far from public corpora — rare on the exa","keywords":["jumpstart","pretrained","fine","tune"]},{"url":"modeling/jumpstart-and-transfer.html#vs-bedrock","page":"JumpStart & transfer learning","section":"JumpStart vs Bedrock","tool":"modeling","text":"02 JumpStart vs Bedrock JumpStart = you own training jobs, containers, endpoints, and classic ML/ DL lifecycle on SageMaker. Bedrock = invoke foundation models via API; Knowledge Bases, Agents, Guardrails — least ops for GenAI text. Choose JumpStart when… Custom vision/tabular DL with your weights M","keywords":["jumpstart","vs","bedrock"]},{"url":"modeling/jumpstart-and-transfer.html#quiz","page":"JumpStart & transfer learning","section":"Exam quiz","tool":"modeling","text":"03 Exam quiz Acme Health wants a nurse-facing chatbot answering policy PDFs with citations, minimal ML ops. Best path? Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base + FM — not JumpStart training from scratch. JumpStart image model fine-tuned on PDF bytes. Train a transformer from random init on policy text. Right. ","keywords":["exam","quiz"]},{"url":"modeling/jumpstart-and-transfer.html#interview","page":"JumpStart & transfer learning","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"modeling","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Fine-tune vs from scratch — default exam pick? warm-up Model answer Fine-tune pretrained (JumpStart or FM) unless the stem explicitly requires training a novel architecture on massive private data with no suitable base model. Say it out loud say it \"Transfer learning first; scratc","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"genai/bedrock-models.html","page":"Bedrock foundation models","section":"Pick the FM: latency, cost, capability.","tool":"genai","text":"Pick the FM: latency, cost, capability. Bedrock exposes multiple foundation models behind one API. MLA-C02 stems force trade-offs — smallest model that meets quality, on-demand vs provisioned throughput, and whether the model is available in your Region.","keywords":["pick","the","fm","latency","cost","capability"]},{"url":"genai/bedrock-models.html#chooser","page":"Bedrock foundation models","section":"Model choice triangle","tool":"genai","text":"01 Model choice triangle Read the stem for latency (real-time chat vs batch), cost (token volume), and capability (reasoning, long context, multimodal). Default to the smallest model that satisfies the hardest constraint — upgrade only when quality fails in eval. flowchart TD need[\"User need\"] --> l","keywords":["model","choice","triangle"]},{"url":"genai/bedrock-models.html#throughput","page":"Bedrock foundation models","section":"On-demand vs provisioned throughput","tool":"genai","text":"02 On-demand vs provisioned throughput On-demand — pay per token, no commitment; best for spiky or POC traffic. Provisioned throughput — reserved model capacity for predictable high QPS and latency SLAs; exam when stem says “consistent sub-second at thousands of TPS.” On-demand Variable traffic, dev","keywords":["on","demand","vs","provisioned","throughput"]},{"url":"genai/bedrock-models.html#regions","page":"Bedrock foundation models","section":"Regions & availability","tool":"genai","text":"03 Regions & availability Not every FM exists in every Region. Stems citing data residency (EU, HIPAA) → invoke in-region; if a model isn’t offered, pick an available alternative or enable cross-Region inference only when the stem allows data movement. Ledger Bank needs 5k requests/minute with stabl","keywords":["regions","availability"]},{"url":"genai/bedrock-models.html#interview","page":"Bedrock foundation models","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"genai","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire How do you pick between two Bedrock models on the exam? core Model answer Smallest model that meets quality, latency, and Region constraints — then compare on-demand vs provisioned from traffic pattern. Say it out loud say it \"Right-size the model first; provisioned only when traf","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"genai/prompt-vs-finetune.html","page":"Prompt vs PEFT / fine-tune","section":"Climb the ladder: prompt → RAG → PEFT.","tool":"genai","text":"Climb the ladder: prompt → RAG → PEFT. Start with the cheapest intervention that meets quality. MLA-C02 rewards the escalation ladder — don’t fine-tune when a system prompt fixes tone, don’t RAG when the answer is in the prompt context.","keywords":["climb","the","ladder","prompt","rag","peft"]},{"url":"genai/prompt-vs-finetune.html#ladder","page":"Prompt vs PEFT / fine-tune","section":"The intervention ladder","tool":"genai","text":"01 The intervention ladder Prompt engineering shapes behavior without weight changes. RAG grounds answers in fresh private docs. PEFT / fine-tune updates weights when style, format, or domain language must be baked in and prompt+RAG plateau. flowchart BT ft[\"PEFT / fine-tune\"] rag[\"RAG + KB\"] prompt","keywords":["the","intervention","ladder"]},{"url":"genai/prompt-vs-finetune.html#when","page":"Prompt vs PEFT / fine-tune","section":"When each wins (exam cues)","tool":"genai","text":"02 When each wins (exam cues) Stop at prompt when… Facts already in context window Behavior/style tweak only POC or “fastest path” Zero private training data policy Escalate to RAG / fine-tune when… Dynamic corpus too large for prompt Hallucination on proprietary facts → RAG Fixed output schema/lang","keywords":["when","each","wins","exam","cues"]},{"url":"genai/prompt-vs-finetune.html#quiz","page":"Prompt vs PEFT / fine-tune","section":"Exam quiz","tool":"genai","text":"03 Exam quiz Chatbot hallucinates drug interactions not in the FM’s public training. Policies live in internal PDFs updated weekly. Next step? Bedrock Knowledge Base (RAG) over the PDF corpus — not full fine-tune. Full fine-tune on all PDFs every week. Longer system prompt copying entire formulary b","keywords":["exam","quiz"]},{"url":"genai/prompt-vs-finetune.html#interview","page":"Prompt vs PEFT / fine-tune","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"genai","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Order: prompt, RAG, fine-tune — why? core Model answer Cheapest and fastest first: prompts need no retraining; RAG adds grounding without weight changes; fine-tune is last when behavior must be in weights and cheaper methods failed. Say it out loud say it \"Prompt, then RAG for fac","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"genai/knowledge-bases-rag.html","page":"Knowledge Bases & RAG design","section":"Design RAG that survives the exam.","tool":"genai","text":"Design RAG that survives the exam. Bedrock Knowledge Bases ingest S3 (and connectors), chunk, embed, and retrieve at query time. MLA stems test chunking, metadata filters, vector store choice, and when hybrid search beats pure vectors.","keywords":["design","rag","that","survives","the","exam"]},{"url":"genai/knowledge-bases-rag.html#flow","page":"Knowledge Bases & RAG design","section":"Knowledge Base RAG path","tool":"genai","text":"01 Knowledge Base RAG path Documents → chunk → embed → vector store → at query time retrieve top-k → FM generates grounded answer. Bedrock manages sync, embedding model, and Retrieve API for custom orchestration. Bedrock KB retrieve-and-generate ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× User → KB retrieve → vector s","keywords":["knowledge","base","rag","path"]},{"url":"genai/knowledge-bases-rag.html#design","page":"Knowledge Bases & RAG design","section":"Chunking, metadata & vector store","tool":"genai","text":"02 Chunking, metadata & vector store Chunk size — smaller for precise FAQ; larger for narrative policy sections. Use metadata filters (department, doc version, effective date) so nurses only see Acme formulary, not research drafts. Vector store — OpenSearch Serverless, Aurora pgvector, Pinecone, etc","keywords":["chunking","metadata","vector","store","department","nursing","status","approved","region","EMEA"]},{"url":"genai/knowledge-bases-rag.html#quiz","page":"Knowledge Bases & RAG design","section":"Exam quiz","tool":"genai","text":"03 Exam quiz Users must only query approved 2026 policies, not archived drafts in the same bucket. Best KB feature? Metadata filters on ingest (e.g. year=2026 , approved=true ) at retrieve time. Delete drafts from S3 permanently before every question. Rely on the FM to ignore old docs without retrie","keywords":["exam","quiz","year","approved","true"]},{"url":"genai/knowledge-bases-rag.html#interview","page":"Knowledge Bases & RAG design","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"genai","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire When hybrid search over pure vector? core Model answer When queries include exact identifiers, SKUs, legal cites, or rare tokens semantic search alone misses — combine keyword BM25 with embeddings. Say it out loud say it \"Exact codes or IDs in the question → hybrid search, not vec","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"genai/agents-and-tools.html","page":"Bedrock Agents & action groups","section":"Agents when the model must act, not just talk.","tool":"genai","text":"Agents when the model must act, not just talk. Amazon Bedrock Agents orchestrate FM reasoning with action groups (OpenAPI → Lambda) and optional Knowledge Bases. MLA-C02: use Agents for multi-step tool use; plain InvokeModel when a single FM call plus RAG is enough.","keywords":["agents","when","the","model","must","act","not","just","talk"]},{"url":"genai/agents-and-tools.html#architecture","page":"Bedrock Agents & action groups","section":"Agents, action groups & Lambda tools","tool":"genai","text":"01 Agents, action groups & Lambda tools An Agent plans steps, calls action groups backed by Lambda (book appointment, check balance), and can attach a Knowledge Base for retrieval — all with guardrails and session memory within the agent runtime. flowchart LR user[\"User\"] --> agent[\"Bedrock Agent\"] ","keywords":["agents","action","groups","lambda","tools"]},{"url":"genai/agents-and-tools.html#when","page":"Bedrock Agents & action groups","section":"Agent vs plain InvokeModel","tool":"genai","text":"02 Agent vs plain InvokeModel InvokeModel (+ optional Retrieve API) when one-shot Q&A or summarization. Agent when the stem needs sequential tool calls, parameter extraction from natural language, or orchestration across KB + APIs with minimal custom code. InvokeModel enough Single-turn FAQ with RAG","keywords":["agent","vs","plain","invokemodel"]},{"url":"genai/agents-and-tools.html#quiz","page":"Bedrock Agents & action groups","section":"Exam quiz","tool":"genai","text":"03 Exam quiz Customer chatbot must quote payoff amount from core banking API after explaining terms from PDFs. Minimal custom orchestration? Bedrock Agent with Knowledge Base + action group Lambda calling the banking API. InvokeModel only — FM guesses balance from training data. Train XGBoost on acc","keywords":["exam","quiz"]},{"url":"genai/agents-and-tools.html#interview","page":"Bedrock Agents & action groups","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"genai","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire What connects an Agent to your backend? core Model answer Action groups defined with OpenAPI schemas targeting Lambda functions that call internal APIs with IAM-scoped permissions. Say it out loud say it \"OpenAPI action group → Lambda → internal API — that's the Agent tool chain.\"","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"genai/guardrails.html","page":"Guardrails & responsible GenAI","section":"Guardrails before users see output.","tool":"genai","text":"Guardrails before users see output. Amazon Bedrock Guardrails filter harmful content, block topics, redact PII, and enforce responsible GenAI policies on both prompts and responses. MLA-C02 stems tie Guardrails to regulated industries — healthcare and banking stories.","keywords":["guardrails","before","users","see","output"]},{"url":"genai/guardrails.html#features","page":"Guardrails & responsible GenAI","section":"What Guardrails control","tool":"genai","text":"01 What Guardrails control Apply a guardrail to Agents, Knowledge Bases, and InvokeModel flows. Configure denied topics , content filters (hate/violence/sexual), PII redaction/blocking , and word/policy filters — evaluate input and output. flowchart LR prompt[\"User prompt\"] --> gr[\"Guardrails\"] gr -","keywords":["what","guardrails","control"]},{"url":"genai/guardrails.html#exam-stems","page":"Guardrails & responsible GenAI","section":"Responsible GenAI exam stems","tool":"genai","text":"02 Responsible GenAI exam stems When stems mention compliance , PII , harmful content , or brand-safe tone , Guardrails beat prompt-only pleading. Combine with KB grounding — Guardrails don’t fix wrong facts, they block unsafe or non-compliant output. Guardrails handle Topic allow/deny lists PII in ","keywords":["responsible","genai","exam","stems"]},{"url":"genai/guardrails.html#quiz","page":"Guardrails & responsible GenAI","section":"Exam quiz","tool":"genai","text":"03 Exam quiz Nurse chatbot must never return patient SSNs even if they appear in pasted notes. Central policy across Agent and standalone app. Service? Bedrock Guardrails with PII detection/redaction on input and output. Longer system prompt saying “please don’t show SSN.” SageMaker Clarify SHAP onl","keywords":["exam","quiz"]},{"url":"genai/guardrails.html#interview","page":"Guardrails & responsible GenAI","section":"Exam rapid-fire","tool":"genai","text":"04 Exam rapid-fire Guardrails vs Knowledge Base — division of labor? core Model answer KB grounds facts from your docs; Guardrails block/redact unsafe topics and PII on prompts and responses. Use both in regulated chatbots. Say it out loud say it \"RAG for truth; Guardrails for safety and compliance ","keywords":["exam","rapid","fire"]},{"url":"deploy/inference-options.html","page":"Inference options","section":"Real-time, serverless, async, or batch — pick the invoke pattern.","tool":"deploy","text":"Real-time, serverless, async, or batch — pick the invoke pattern. MLA-C02 loves the latency vs cost vs payload triangle. SageMaker offers four primary invoke paths; Bedrock is its own managed FM layer. As of September 2026, match the stem’s SLA before you name an instance type.","keywords":["real","time","serverless","async","or","batch","pick","the","invoke","pattern"]},{"url":"deploy/inference-options.html#chooser","page":"Inference options","section":"Four SageMaker invoke patterns","tool":"deploy","text":"01 Four SageMaker invoke patterns Ask: Does the caller wait? How big is the payload? How steady is traffic? Wrong pattern = idle GPU bills or timeout failures. flowchart TD start[\"Need predictions?\"] --> wait{\"Caller waits?\"} wait -->|yes · ms SLA| rt{\"Traffic steady?\"} rt -->|yes · always on| real[","keywords":["four","sagemaker","invoke","patterns"]},{"url":"deploy/inference-options.html#patterns","page":"Inference options","section":"Pattern cheat sheet","tool":"deploy","text":"02 Pattern cheat sheet Real-time endpoint HTTPS invoke, ms–low-sec latency Multi-variant / multi-model endpoints for A/B Auto scaling on invocations & GPU util Exam: “API must respond in real time, steady load” Serverless Inference Managed concurrency, scale-to-zero Cold-start penalty after idle No ","keywords":["pattern","cheat","sheet"]},{"url":"deploy/inference-options.html#acme","page":"Inference options","section":"Acme Health — readmission API vs nightly cohort","tool":"deploy","text":"03 Acme Health — readmission API vs nightly cohort Acme serves real-time risk scores at discharge (endpoint) and runs a nightly batch over the whole census (Batch Transform). Same model artifact — two invoke modes. Common trap gotcha Don’t put a real-time endpoint on a “run once per night over 2M ro","keywords":["acme","health","readmission","api","vs","nightly","cohort"]},{"url":"deploy/inference-options.html#bedrock","page":"Inference options","section":"Bedrock vs SageMaker endpoint","tool":"deploy","text":"04 Bedrock vs SageMaker endpoint Bedrock = managed FM APIs (invoke, streaming, KB RAG). SageMaker endpoint = your custom container or built-in algorithm. Stem mentions “foundation model” or “Claude/Llama on AWS” → Bedrock first. Bedrock FM inference + KB + Agents Token pricing, no GPU ops Guardrails","keywords":["bedrock","vs","sagemaker","endpoint"]},{"url":"deploy/inference-options.html#check","page":"Inference options","section":"Rapid-fire","tool":"deploy","text":"05 Rapid-fire Ledger Bank fraud model: traffic spikes 9–10 a.m., quiet overnight. Minimize idle cost, 200 ms p99 OK after warm-up. Deploy Answer Serverless Inference — scale to zero; real-time endpoint wastes GPU overnight. Score 500 GB of loan applications this weekend; no online API. Deploy Answer","keywords":["rapid","fire"]},{"url":"deploy/model-registry.html","page":"Model Registry & promotion","section":"Version, approve, promote — govern what reaches production.","tool":"deploy","text":"Version, approve, promote — govern what reaches production. Model Registry is SageMaker’s system of record for model packages: lineage from training job, approval gates, and promotion to endpoints. MLA-C02 stems about “only approved models in prod” or “rollback to last good version” land here.","keywords":["version","approve","promote","govern","what","reaches","production"]},{"url":"deploy/model-registry.html#concepts","page":"Model Registry & promotion","section":"Registry building blocks","tool":"deploy","text":"01 Registry building blocks A model group holds versions. Each model package points at an approved artifact (S3 model.tar.gz or image). Status flows: PendingManualApproval → Approved / Rejected . flowchart LR train[\"Training job\"] --> pkg[\"Model package vN\"] pkg --> reg[\"Model Registry group\"] reg -","keywords":["registry","building","blocks","acme","readmission","xgb"]},{"url":"deploy/model-registry.html#promotion","page":"Model Registry & promotion","section":"Promotion workflow","tool":"deploy","text":"02 Promotion workflow Production deploy should never pull arbitrary S3 paths. CI/CD reads the latest Approved package in the group — or a pinned version for canary. 1 Register Training job completes → register model package (PendingManualApproval). 2 Evaluate Offline metrics + Clarify bias report at","keywords":["promotion","workflow"]},{"url":"deploy/model-registry.html#ledger","page":"Model Registry & promotion","section":"Ledger Bank — fraud model governance","tool":"deploy","text":"03 Ledger Bank — fraud model governance Ledger runs three fraud model versions in Registry. Only Approved packages feed the payment endpoint. When drift triggers retrain, v47 registers pending until the risk committee approves — v46 stays live. With Registry Clear version history & rollback Approval","keywords":["ledger","bank","fraud","model","governance","s3","models","latest"]},{"url":"deploy/model-registry.html#multi","page":"Model Registry & promotion","section":"Multi-model & A/B endpoints","tool":"deploy","text":"04 Multi-model & A/B endpoints Multi-model endpoints host several approved packages on shared instances. Production variants split traffic (80/20) for champion/challenger — still sourced from Registry versions. Common trap gotcha Model Registry ≠ Model Monitor. Registry governs which artifact deploy","keywords":["multi","model","endpoints"]},{"url":"deploy/model-registry.html#check","page":"Model Registry & promotion","section":"Rapid-fire","tool":"deploy","text":"05 Rapid-fire New model fails offline AUC gate. What happens to prod? Deploy Answer Package stays Rejected or pending; pipeline does not update endpoint. Previous Approved version continues serving. Auditors need “which training job produced prod model on March 3?” Deploy Answer Model Registry linea","keywords":["rapid","fire"]},{"url":"deploy/pipelines-cicd.html","page":"Pipelines & CI/CD for ML","section":"Automate retrain, evaluate, approve, deploy.","tool":"deploy","text":"Automate retrain, evaluate, approve, deploy. SageMaker Pipelines is the native ML workflow engine. Pair it with CodePipeline/CodeBuild for Git-triggered CI, or EventBridge for scheduled retraining. MLA-C02 rewards repeatable pipelines over one-off notebook deploys.","keywords":["automate","retrain","evaluate","approve","deploy"]},{"url":"deploy/pipelines-cicd.html#pipeline","page":"Pipelines & CI/CD for ML","section":"SageMaker Pipelines anatomy","tool":"deploy","text":"01 SageMaker Pipelines anatomy A pipeline is a DAG of steps : Processing, Training, Evaluation, Condition, RegisterModel, CreateModel, Transform. Parameters make runs reusable; caching skips unchanged steps. flowchart LR proc[\"Processing\"] --> train[\"Training\"] train --> eval[\"Evaluation\"] eval --> ","keywords":["sagemaker","pipelines","anatomy"]},{"url":"deploy/pipelines-cicd.html#cicd","page":"Pipelines & CI/CD for ML","section":"CI/CD integration cues","tool":"deploy","text":"02 CI/CD integration cues Typical pattern: CodeCommit/GitHub → CodePipeline → CodeBuild (lint/test) → StartPipelineExecution on SageMaker Pipelines. Infrastructure as Code via CloudFormation or CDK for roles and buckets. Ledger Bank · retrain on drift ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× Monitor → EventBridge →","keywords":["ci","cd","integration","cues"]},{"url":"deploy/pipelines-cicd.html#triggers","page":"Pipelines & CI/CD for ML","section":"Automated retraining triggers","tool":"deploy","text":"03 Automated retraining triggers Schedule EventBridge cron — weekly retrain Good for stable domains May waste compute if no drift Event-driven Model Monitor / CloudWatch alarm New labeled data in S3 (EventBridge) Retrain only when needed Common trap gotcha “Automate ML workflow” → SageMaker Pipeline","keywords":["automated","retraining","triggers"]},{"url":"deploy/pipelines-cicd.html#check","page":"Pipelines & CI/CD for ML","section":"Rapid-fire","tool":"deploy","text":"04 Rapid-fire Skip retrain if validation AUC drops below champion. Which step? Deploy Answer ConditionStep gates RegisterModel — failed branch stops deploy. Git push should kick off full ML pipeline. Minimal add-on? Deploy Answer CodePipeline on repo change → CodeBuild tests → Lambda/start pipeline ","keywords":["rapid","fire"]},{"url":"deploy/orchestrate-genai.html","page":"Orchestrate GenAI workloads","section":"Agents, Step Functions, and multi-step FM workflows.","tool":"deploy","text":"Agents, Step Functions, and multi-step FM workflows. GenAI deploy is more than InvokeModel . Bedrock Agents orchestrate tool use; Step Functions coordinate long flows with retries, parallelism, and cost caps. MLA-C02 tests when to compose services vs call Bedrock once.","keywords":["agents","step","functions","and","multi","fm","workflows"]},{"url":"deploy/orchestrate-genai.html#agents","page":"Orchestrate GenAI workloads","section":"Bedrock Agents & action groups","tool":"deploy","text":"01 Bedrock Agents & action groups An Agent plans multi-turn tasks: FM reasons → calls action groups (OpenAPI → Lambda) → returns grounded answers. Knowledge Bases supply RAG retrieval without you wiring vector DB manually. flowchart LR user[\"User query\"] --> agent[\"Bedrock Agent\"] agent --> kb[\"Know","keywords":["bedrock","agents","action","groups"]},{"url":"deploy/orchestrate-genai.html#stepfn","page":"Orchestrate GenAI workloads","section":"Step Functions + Bedrock","tool":"deploy","text":"02 Step Functions + Bedrock Use Step Functions when you need explicit control : parallel doc summarization, human approval, exponential backoff, or mixing Bedrock with SageMaker endpoints and DynamoDB. flowchart TD start[\"Start\"] --> split[\"Parallel map\"] split --> b1[\"Bedrock summarize doc 1\"] spli","keywords":["step","functions","bedrock"]},{"url":"deploy/orchestrate-genai.html#cost","page":"Orchestrate GenAI workloads","section":"Concurrency & cost cues","tool":"deploy","text":"03 Concurrency & cost cues Bedrock bills per token; unbounded parallel invokes explode cost. Cap concurrency with Step Functions Map state MaxConcurrency , SQS throttling, or reserved throughput where offered. Acme clinician assistant tip Acme caps Agent loops at 5 tool calls per session and routes ","keywords":["concurrency","cost","cues"]},{"url":"deploy/orchestrate-genai.html#check","page":"Orchestrate GenAI workloads","section":"Rapid-fire","tool":"deploy","text":"04 Rapid-fire Chatbot must call HR API and search policy PDFs — least custom orchestration code. Deploy Answer Bedrock Agent with Knowledge Base + action group Lambda for HR API. Summarize 10k loan files nightly with human sign-off on exceptions only. Deploy Answer Step Functions Map + Bedrock invok","keywords":["rapid","fire"]},{"url":"ops-security/monitor-and-drift.html","page":"Monitor & drift","section":"Catch data drift, quality decay, and endpoint failures early.","tool":"ops-security","text":"Catch data drift, quality decay, and endpoint failures early. Model Monitor schedules baseline comparisons on live endpoint traffic. CloudWatch tracks latency, errors, and custom metrics. MLA-C02 ties “model degraded in production” to Monitor + alarms + retrain pipeline — not manual eyeballing.","keywords":["catch","data","drift","quality","decay","and","endpoint","failures","early"]},{"url":"ops-security/monitor-and-drift.html#monitor","page":"Monitor & drift","section":"SageMaker Model Monitor","tool":"ops-security","text":"01 SageMaker Model Monitor Enable data capture on the endpoint → Monitor builds baselines from training data → scheduled jobs compare live distributions and optional model quality (labels required). flowchart LR ep[\"Endpoint\"] --> cap[\"Data capture\"] cap --> s3[\"S3 monitoring folder\"] s3 --> job[\"Mo","keywords":["sagemaker","model","monitor"]},{"url":"ops-security/monitor-and-drift.html#cloudwatch","page":"Monitor & drift","section":"CloudWatch for endpoints","tool":"ops-security","text":"02 CloudWatch for endpoints Built-in metrics: Invocations , ModelLatency , 4XX/5XX , CPUUtilization , GPUUtilization . Set alarms on latency spikes and error rates — distinct from statistical drift. CloudWatch Ops health: down, slow, throttling Auto scaling triggers Logs via CloudWatch Logs Model Mo","keywords":["cloudwatch","for","endpoints","Invocations","ModelLatency","XX","CPUUtilization","GPUUtilization"]},{"url":"ops-security/monitor-and-drift.html#acme","page":"Monitor & drift","section":"Acme Health — readmission drift","tool":"ops-security","text":"03 Acme Health — readmission drift After a new EHR field rollout, length_of_stay distribution shifts. Model Monitor flags data drift; Acme’s EventBridge rule starts the retrain pipeline before AUC collapses. 1 Baseline Create baseline from training manifest at deploy time. 2 Capture Endpoint capture","keywords":["acme","health","readmission","drift","length_of_stay"]},{"url":"ops-security/monitor-and-drift.html#genai","page":"Monitor & drift","section":"GenAI monitoring cues","tool":"ops-security","text":"04 GenAI monitoring cues Bedrock: enable model invocation logging to S3/CloudWatch; use Guardrails for blocked prompts/responses; track token usage via CloudWatch. RAG quality often needs custom eval (human or LLM-as-judge) — not classic Monitor baselines.","keywords":["genai","monitoring","cues"]},{"url":"ops-security/monitor-and-drift.html#check","page":"Monitor & drift","section":"Rapid-fire","tool":"ops-security","text":"05 Rapid-fire Feature distributions changed; predictions still within SLA latency. Operate Answer Model Monitor data drift job — not CloudWatch latency alone. Endpoint returns 503 under load. First check? Operate Answer CloudWatch invocations/errors + auto scaling policy / instance capacity.","keywords":["rapid","fire"]},{"url":"ops-security/cost-and-accelerators.html","page":"Cost & accelerators","section":"Right-size training, cheap inference, smart FM spend.","tool":"ops-security","text":"Right-size training, cheap inference, smart FM spend. ML bills come from always-on endpoints , oversized training instances , and unbounded Bedrock tokens . MLA-C02 pairs cost constraints with the right accelerator or pricing model — Spot for fault-tolerant train, Inferentia for inference, token cap","keywords":["right","size","training","cheap","inference","smart","fm","spend"]},{"url":"ops-security/cost-and-accelerators.html#training","page":"Cost & accelerators","section":"Training cost levers","tool":"ops-security","text":"01 Training cost levers Use Spot Training with checkpointing for long jobs. Right-size instance family; use managed Spot in SageMaker Training. HPO parallel jobs multiply cost — cap concurrent trials. On-Demand training Predictable, no interruption Higher $/hour Short jobs / final prod train Spot / ","keywords":["training","cost","levers"]},{"url":"ops-security/cost-and-accelerators.html#accelerators","page":"Cost & accelerators","section":"Inferentia, Trainium & GPU","tool":"ops-security","text":"02 Inferentia, Trainium & GPU AWS AI chips: Trainium for training throughput, Inferentia/Inferentia2 for low-cost inference on SageMaker and EC2. Exam stem “reduce inference cost at scale” → Neo compile + Inf instances before defaulting to p4d GPU. flowchart TD need[\"Cut inference $\"] --> custom{\"Cu","keywords":["inferentia","trainium","gpu"]},{"url":"ops-security/cost-and-accelerators.html#bedrock","page":"Cost & accelerators","section":"Bedrock token economics","tool":"ops-security","text":"03 Bedrock token economics On-demand = pay per input/output token. Provisioned throughput when steady high volume. Reduce tokens: shorter prompts, RAG top-k limits, caching (where supported), smaller FM for routing tasks. Ledger Bank tip Ledger routes simple intent classification to a small FM and e","keywords":["bedrock","token","economics"]},{"url":"ops-security/cost-and-accelerators.html#endpoint","page":"Cost & accelerators","section":"Endpoint right-sizing","tool":"ops-security","text":"04 Endpoint right-sizing Over-provision trap ml.p3.2xlarge 24/7 for 10 req/min Serverless or smaller Inf instance Fix Auto scaling on InvocationsPerInstance Multi-model endpoint share GPU Batch for offline scoring","keywords":["endpoint","right","sizing"]},{"url":"ops-security/cost-and-accelerators.html#check","page":"Cost & accelerators","section":"Rapid-fire","tool":"ops-security","text":"05 Rapid-fire 12-hour HPO with checkpointing; minimize train cost. Operate Answer Managed Spot Training with S3 checkpointing on interrupt. High-QPS XGBoost inference; GPU idle most of the day. Operate Answer Graviton CPU or Inferentia after Neo — or Serverless Inference if spiky.","keywords":["rapid","fire"]},{"url":"ops-security/iam-for-ml.html","page":"IAM for training & endpoints","section":"Execution roles for every ML job — no long-lived keys.","tool":"ops-security","text":"Execution roles for every ML job — no long-lived keys. SageMaker jobs and endpoints assume IAM execution roles with least-privilege policies. Training reads S3; endpoints invoke nothing unless you allow it. MLA-C02 punishes access keys in notebooks and overly broad s3:* on production roles.","keywords":["execution","roles","for","every","ml","job","no","long","lived","keys"]},{"url":"ops-security/iam-for-ml.html#roles","page":"IAM for training & endpoints","section":"Role per workload","tool":"ops-security","text":"01 Role per workload Separate roles for Studio users , training , processing , endpoint , and Pipeline execution. Each gets only the S3 prefixes, KMS keys, and ECR repos it needs. flowchart TB subgraph roles[\"Execution roles\"] tr[\"Training role\"] pr[\"Processing role\"] ep[\"Endpoint role\"] pl[\"Pipelin","keywords":["role","per","workload"]},{"url":"ops-security/iam-for-ml.html#least","page":"IAM for training & endpoints","section":"Least privilege patterns","tool":"ops-security","text":"02 Least privilege patterns Do Resource-scoped S3 ARNs per prefix KMS via key policy + role Condition keys: vpc, source account Separate dev vs prod roles Don’t IAM user keys in Studio notebook s3:* on entire account Same role for train and human admin Star actions on sagemaker:* PassRole trap gotch","keywords":["least","privilege","patterns","s3","sagemaker","iam","PassRole"]},{"url":"ops-security/iam-for-ml.html#bedrock","page":"IAM for training & endpoints","section":"Bedrock & Lambda IAM","tool":"ops-security","text":"03 Bedrock & Lambda IAM Bedrock invoke from Lambda/Agent: role needs bedrock:InvokeModel on allowed FM ARNs. Knowledge Bases add OpenSearch/S3/Vectors permissions. Use resource-based policies where Bedrock writes to your bucket. Acme · scoped endpoint role ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× Endpoint assumes r","keywords":["bedrock","lambda","iam","InvokeModel"]},{"url":"ops-security/iam-for-ml.html#check","page":"IAM for training & endpoints","section":"Rapid-fire","tool":"ops-security","text":"04 Rapid-fire Notebook uses hard-coded IAM user keys to read S3. Fix? Operate Answer Studio execution role or SSO user role with scoped S3 — rotate and delete keys. Pipeline cannot launch Training job — role error on PassRole. Operate Answer Grant pipeline role iam:PassRole to the training execution","keywords":["rapid","fire","iam","PassRole"]},{"url":"ops-security/encryption-and-lineage.html","page":"Encryption & lineage","section":"Encrypt artifacts in flight and at rest; prove where models came from.","tool":"ops-security","text":"Encrypt artifacts in flight and at rest; prove where models came from. KMS wraps S3 model artifacts, EBS volumes on training instances, and optional endpoint encryption in transit. SageMaker Lineage and Model Cards document intent, metrics, and risks for auditors. MLA-C02 merges security with respon","keywords":["encrypt","artifacts","in","flight","and","at","rest","prove","where","models","came","from"]},{"url":"ops-security/encryption-and-lineage.html#kms","page":"Encryption & lineage","section":"KMS for ML data paths","tool":"ops-security","text":"01 KMS for ML data paths Default SSE-S3 works for many labs; production stems ask for SSE-KMS with customer managed keys (CMKs), separate keys per env, and least-privilege key policies for training vs endpoint roles. flowchart LR data[\"S3 datasets SSE-KMS\"] --> train[\"Training job\"] train --> art[\"M","keywords":["kms","for","ml","data","paths"]},{"url":"ops-security/encryption-and-lineage.html#lineage","page":"Encryption & lineage","section":"Lineage & Model Cards","tool":"ops-security","text":"02 Lineage & Model Cards Lineage tracks entities: data source → processing job → training job → model package → endpoint. Model Cards (often with Clarify) capture intended use, limitations, bias eval, and contact — the human-readable audit doc. Lineage graph Automatic from Pipelines Query “what data","keywords":["lineage","model","cards"]},{"url":"ops-security/encryption-and-lineage.html#clarify","page":"Encryption & lineage","section":"Clarify in the governance story","tool":"ops-security","text":"03 Clarify in the governance story Clarify runs pre-training (data bias), post-training (model bias, SHAP explainability), and can integrate with Monitor for post-deploy bias drift. Lineage stores clarify job outputs as artifacts.","keywords":["clarify","in","the","governance","story"]},{"url":"ops-security/encryption-and-lineage.html#check","page":"Encryption & lineage","section":"Rapid-fire","tool":"ops-security","text":"04 Rapid-fire Endpoint cannot download model — AccessDenied on KMS Decrypt. Operate Answer Add endpoint execution role to KMS key policy with kms:Decrypt . Prove which S3 dataset version trained production model 9. Operate Answer SageMaker Lineage from model package back to dataset artifact — plus R","keywords":["rapid","fire","kms","Decrypt"]},{"url":"exam/domain-map.html","page":"MLA-C02 domain map","section":"Four domains. One ML engineering brain.","tool":"exam","text":"Four domains. One ML engineering brain. MLA-C02 scores you on Data preparation · Model/FM development · Deploy & orchestrate · Operate, monitor, secure . This map shows which guide lessons feed each domain — study by domain when a mock exposes a weak area. As of September 2026.","keywords":["four","domains","one","ml","engineering","brain"]},{"url":"exam/domain-map.html#domains","page":"MLA-C02 domain map","section":"Official domains at a glance","tool":"exam","text":"01 Official domains at a glance Domain Weight (approx.) What \"good\" looks like 1 · Data preparation for ML ~28% Ingest, transform, feature engineering, Feature Store, embeddings for RAG, integrity/bias/leakage 2 · ML model & FM development ~24% Algorithm choice, SageMaker training, HPO, evaluation, ","keywords":["official","domains","at","glance"]},{"url":"exam/domain-map.html#map","page":"MLA-C02 domain map","section":"Domains → lessons","tool":"exam","text":"02 Domains → lessons flowchart TB subgraph d1[\"Domain 1 · Data\"] ingest[ingest-and-store] transform[transform-and-features] fs[feature-store] embed[embeddings-for-rag] end subgraph d2[\"Domain 2 · Model\"] train[train-on-sagemaker] hpo[hpo-and-tuning] eval[evaluate-and-clarify] genai[bedrock-models] e","keywords":["domains","lessons"]},{"url":"exam/domain-map.html#method","page":"MLA-C02 domain map","section":"How to use this map","tool":"exam","text":"03 How to use this map Finish Exam method so you know how stems hide the domain. Run the timed mock — note weak domain bars. Drill that domain's lessons, then the scenario bank filtered by domain chip. Night-before: cheat-sheet + flashcards .","keywords":["how","to","use","this","map"]},{"url":"exam/domain-map.html#check","page":"MLA-C02 domain map","section":"Quick self-check","tool":"exam","text":"04 Quick self-check Stem: \"training and inference features must match; low-latency serving.\" Domain? warm-up Answer Data (Domain 1) — Feature Store online/offline consistency. Deploy appears only if stem asks endpoint type. Stem: \"only approved models reach production endpoint.\" Domain? core Answer ","keywords":["quick","self","check"]},{"url":"exam/scenario-bank.html","page":"ML scenario bank","section":"Reason before you invoke.","tool":"exam","text":"Reason before you invoke. 14 architecture stems across Data · Model · Deploy · Operate . Cards marked Flagship walkthrough teach the exam method: underline the constraint, kill two distractors, lock the answer. Compact cards are drill. Acme Health and Ledger Bank stories throughout. September 2026.","keywords":["reason","before","you","invoke"]},{"url":"exam/scenario-bank.html#data","page":"ML scenario bank","section":"Data preparation","tool":"exam","text":"01 Data preparation Acme EHR features must match between batch train and real-time scoring Data Flagship walkthrough Story — Acme engineers rebuilt features differently in the training notebook vs the Lambda pre-processing hack. Readmission scores drifted in prod. Underline constraint — Same feature","keywords":["data","preparation"]},{"url":"exam/scenario-bank.html#model","page":"ML scenario bank","section":"Model & FM development","tool":"exam","text":"02 Model & FM development Acme wants best XGBoost hyperparameters without manual grid search Model Flagship walkthrough Underline constraint — Automated tuning on SageMaker with budget cap. Best answer — SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) — Bayesian or random search across Training jobs; objecti","keywords":["model","fm","development"]},{"url":"exam/scenario-bank.html#deploy","page":"ML scenario bank","section":"Deploy & orchestrate","tool":"exam","text":"03 Deploy & orchestrate Ledger fraud API: spiky traffic, scale to zero overnight OK Deploy Flagship walkthrough Underline constraint — Minimize idle cost ; 200 ms p99 after warm-up acceptable. Best answer — Serverless Inference endpoint (or Bedrock if FM). Not always-on GPU real-time endpoint. Why —","keywords":["deploy","orchestrate"]},{"url":"exam/scenario-bank.html#operate","page":"ML scenario bank","section":"Operate, monitor, secure","tool":"exam","text":"04 Operate, monitor, secure Acme readmission features drift after EHR upgrade — automate response Operate Flagship walkthrough Best answer — Model Monitor data drift baseline → CloudWatch alarm → EventBridge → SageMaker Pipeline retrain. Why — Statistical drift = Monitor, not CloudWatch CPU alone. f","keywords":["operate","monitor","secure","kms","Decrypt"]},{"url":"exam/mock-exam.html","page":"Timed mock exam","section":"35-minute MLA-C02 mock.","tool":"exam","text":"35-minute MLA-C02 mock. Twenty-two domain-weighted questions (~6 Data, ~5 Model, ~5 Deploy, ~6 Operate). Start the timer, pick one answer each, then score — each question shows why and a lesson link . Acme Health & Ledger Bank scenarios. September 2026.","keywords":["minute","mla","c02","mock"]},{"url":"exam/mock-exam.html#take","page":"Timed mock exam","section":"Take the mock","tool":"exam","text":"01 Take the mock Treat it like the real exam: no peeking at notes. After scoring, use domain bars and the per-question review to choose the next lesson from the domain map .","keywords":["take","the","mock"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html","page":"MLA cheat-sheet","section":"Night-before MLA choosers.","tool":"exam","text":"Night-before MLA choosers. Dense rapid-recall: inference modes, RAG vs fine-tune, Feature Store, Model Monitor, IAM roles. Skim once; quiz yourself from the left column. MLA-C02 · September 2026.","keywords":["night","before","mla","choosers"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#inference","page":"MLA cheat-sheet","section":"Inference chooser","tool":"exam","text":"01 Inference chooser SageMaker invoke modes Pattern When Real-time endpoint Steady low-latency HTTPS API Serverless Inference Spiky traffic; scale-to-zero OK Async inference Large payload; client polls/SNS Batch Transform Offline bulk S3 scoring Bedrock InvokeModel Managed FM / GenAI API Latency vs ","keywords":["inference","chooser"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#genai","page":"MLA cheat-sheet","section":"RAG vs fine-tune vs prompt","tool":"exam","text":"02 RAG vs fine-tune vs prompt Prompt only Behavior/style tweaks Zero data pipeline Weakest on private facts RAG (Knowledge Base) Ground on your docs Chunk + embed + retrieve Exam first for private knowledge Fine-tune / PEFT Domain tone / task format Needs labeled examples After prompt+RAG insufficie","keywords":["rag","vs","fine","tune","prompt"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#feature-store","page":"MLA cheat-sheet","section":"Feature Store","tool":"exam","text":"03 Feature Store Offline vs online Offline store Historical exports for training/batch — S3-backed. Online store Low-latency key lookup at inference (DynamoDB). Same feature group Prevents train-serve skew. Not Feature Store Raw Glue table alone; ad hoc Lambda features. Data integrity Leakage Future","keywords":["feature","store"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#monitor","page":"MLA cheat-sheet","section":"Model Monitor & ops metrics","tool":"exam","text":"04 Model Monitor & ops metrics Model Monitor Data quality / drift / bias Needs baseline + capture Triggers retrain pipeline CloudWatch Invocations, latency, 4XX/5XX CPU/GPU util, auto scaling Not statistical feature drift Clarify Explainability + bias at train time — not live drift alone. Guardrails","keywords":["model","monitor","ops","metrics"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#iam","page":"MLA cheat-sheet","section":"IAM for ML","tool":"exam","text":"05 IAM for ML Execution roles Training / Processing Read/write scoped S3; PassRole from pipeline. Endpoint Get model artifact + KMS decrypt — minimal write. Pipeline Start jobs, register models, create endpoints. Never Long-lived IAM user keys in notebooks. Registry & pipeline Model Registry Approve","keywords":["iam","for","ml"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#cost","page":"MLA cheat-sheet","section":"Cost & accelerators","tool":"exam","text":"06 Cost & accelerators Lever Use Managed Spot Training Long fault-tolerant train + checkpoint Inferentia + Neo High-throughput/$ inference Graviton CPU classical ML inference Serverless Inference Idle endpoint cost cut Bedrock tokens Shorter prompts, RAG top-k, route small FM","keywords":["cost","accelerators"]},{"url":"exam/cheat-sheet.html#domains","page":"MLA cheat-sheet","section":"Four MLA domains (weights)","tool":"exam","text":"07 Four MLA domains (weights) Data ~28% Ingest, transform, Feature Store, RAG data, leakage. Model ~24% Train, HPO, Clarify, JumpStart, Bedrock FM choice. Deploy ~24% Inference mode, Registry, Pipelines, Agents. Operate ~24% Monitor, IAM, KMS, cost, lineage, Guardrails.","keywords":["four","mla","domains","weights"]},{"url":"exam/flashcards.html","page":"MLA flashcards","section":"Active-recall MLA flashcards.","tool":"exam","text":"Active-recall MLA flashcards. 40 cards across Data, Model, Deploy, and Operate. Tap to flip; mark Known or Review. Say the answer out loud before you peek. MLA-C02 · September 2026.","keywords":["active","recall","mla","flashcards"]},{"url":"exam/flashcards.html#cards","page":"MLA flashcards","section":"The deck","tool":"exam","text":"01 The deck Recall beats re-reading. Try to answer out loud before flipping — that's the muscle MLA-C02 tests. 0 / 0 known Shuffle Reset Four MLA-C02 domains and rough weights? Data prep ~28%, Model/FM ~24%, Deploy ~24%, Operate ~24%. Known Review SageMaker vs Bedrock — when which? SageMaker = your ","keywords":["the","deck"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html","page":"Glossary","section":"MLA glossary, A–Z.","tool":"home","text":"MLA glossary, A–Z. Crisp definitions for Machine Learning Engineer – Associate vocabulary. AWS-first, September 2026. Self-contained for this guide — Acme Health & Ledger Bank contexts where helpful.","keywords":["mla","glossary"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#a-f","page":"Glossary","section":"A – F","tool":"home","text":"01 A – F Action group (Bedrock) OpenAPI-defined tools an Agent can invoke via Lambda — e.g. Ledger leave-balance API. Async inference SageMaker pattern for large payloads: input to S3, process asynchronously, notify via SNS or poll. Automatic Model Tuning (AMT) SageMaker hyperparameter optimization ","keywords":[]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#g-m","page":"Glossary","section":"G – M","tool":"home","text":"02 G – M Guardrails (Bedrock) Policy layer filtering unsafe content, denied topics, and PII in GenAI requests/responses. Hyperparameter optimization (HPO) Automated search over training hyperparameters — SageMaker AMT. Inferentia AWS inference chip (Inf1/Inf2) for cost-efficient high-throughput mode","keywords":[]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#n-z","page":"Glossary","section":"N – Z","tool":"home","text":"03 N – Z Neo (SageMaker) Model compilation for optimized deployment on Inferentia, Graviton, or GPUs. Offline store (Feature Store) S3-backed historical features for training and batch scoring exports. Online store (Feature Store) Low-latency feature lookup store (DynamoDB) for real-time inference. ","keywords":[]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#check","page":"Glossary","section":"Use it","tool":"home","text":"04 Use it Skim before mocks; if a term is fuzzy, jump to its module from the domain map or drill flashcards .","keywords":["use","it"]}];
